+# Carrier Access, Ownership, Charter, Command, and Crew Sovereignty Contract
+
+## Owned Question
+
+**How hard should it be for a player to reach, own, build and control a large carrier while preserving a satisfying progression fantasy and the reality that such a vessel is inhabited and produced by a society?**
+
+## Core Answer
+
+- **Access should begin early.**
+- **Useful operation should require training but remain approachable.**
+- **Department leadership should be a substantial achievement.**
+- **Command should require trust, legitimacy and demonstrated coordination.**
+- **Ownership should normally be shared, financed, chartered or institutional.**
+- **Independent construction should be a civilization-scale campaign.**
+- **Absolute personal control should not exist over autonomous inhabitants.**
+
+> **The player should not wait hundreds of hours to experience a carrier. They should spend hundreds of hours discovering how many different relationships a person can have with one.**
+
+# 1. Five Separate Progression Axes
+
+## 1.1 Access
+
+Can the character enter, reside aboard or use carrier services?
+
+Access can be earned through:
+
+- travel ticket;
+- refugee protection;
+- employment;
+- invitation;
+- household relationship;
+- public service;
+- trade;
+- convoy membership;
+- emergency rescue;
+- citizenship or residency.
+
+Access is the easiest axis and should appear early.
+
+## 1.2 Qualification
+
+Can the character safely operate a station, craft, module or procedure?
+
+Qualification requires:
+
+- instruction;
+- supervised practice;
+- demonstrated competence;
+- current medical or embodiment fitness where relevant;
+- legal recognition;
+- equipment familiarity;
+- periodic renewal for high-risk work.
+
+## 1.3 Authority
+
+May the character decide how a system or department is used?
+
+Authority derives from:
+
+- job role;
+- election;
+- appointment;
+- ownership of a specific module;
+- emergency delegation;
+- mission charter;
+- court or civic process;
+- machine or alien consent.
+
+Authority has scope and expiration.
+
+## 1.4 Ownership and beneficial interest
+
+Who holds title, debt, investment, salvage rights, public trust or future proceeds?
+
+Ownership can be fragmented and disputed.
+
+## 1.5 Command legitimacy
+
+Who may coordinate vessel-wide action under ordinary and emergency conditions?
+
+Command is a political and professional relationship, not the highest ownership tier.
+
+# 2. Recommended Difficulty Curve
+
+## Early game: encounter and contribute
+
+Within the first major planetary or orbital progression, the player should be able to:
+
+- board a carrier;
+- travel aboard it;
+- use public spaces;
+- work a supervised station;
+- bring a mobile home into a carrier berth;
+- meet residents and institutions;
+- participate in one emergency.
+
+This makes the fantasy visible before ownership.
+
+## Midgame: specialize and steward
+
+The player can:
+
+- qualify for several stations;
+- lead a watch or small department;
+- steward a module;
+- operate a carried base;
+- plan deployments;
+- negotiate refits;
+- hold cooperative shares;
+- earn command trust.
+
+## Late midgame: coordinate and charter
+
+The player can:
+
+- become department head;
+- command a mission;
+- serve on the carrier council;
+- captain a medium base-carrier;
+- acquire a major module or minority ownership interest;
+- organize a construction consortium;
+- propose a new carrier charter.
+
+## Late game: build or inherit a civilization hull
+
+The player can:
+
+- coordinate a full carrier build;
+- complete an abandoned hull;
+- lead a major refit;
+- become elected or appointed vessel commander;
+- found a carrier cooperative or public authority;
+- inherit a complex claim;
+- split, merge or transform a carrier polity;
+- commission a seed continuity vessel.
+
+The achievement is not “unlocking the ship.” It is creating the material and social capacity to sustain it.
+
+# 3. Ownership Models
+
+## Public carrier
+
+Owned by a municipality, federation, planetary service or route authority.
+
+Player progression comes through office, expertise, trust and democratic mandate.
+
+## Cooperative carrier
+
+Workers, residents and partner institutions hold shares and governance rights.
+
+Player influence grows by contribution and coalition, not purchase alone.
+
+## Household or lineage carrier
+
+A multi-generational family or house holds title under obligations to residents, creditors and public law.
+
+Inheritance can produce conflict without making bloodline authority absolute.
+
+## Charter consortium
+
+Several settlements, firms, cooperatives and public services finance and govern the carrier.
+
+No party can unilaterally repurpose it.
+
+## Private carrier
+
+A person or company holds substantial title, but resident, worker, environmental and safety rights remain binding.
+
+Private ownership may grant strategic influence and financial risk, not ownership of lives.
+
+## Commons or mission trust
+
+The carrier is legally dedicated to a purpose such as rescue, seed continuity, ecological preservation or route maintenance.
+
+Trustees cannot lawfully liquidate it for unrelated gain.
+
+## Captured or salvaged carrier
+
+Physical possession initiates claims and duties; it does not instantly settle title.
+
+# 4. How Expensive Should Ownership Be?
+
+A medium base-carrier should be comparable to owning a major industrial vessel, district-scale infrastructure or regional institution.
+
+A civilization carrier should normally exceed the personal wealth of an ordinary character by orders of magnitude.
+
+Players can still become central through:
+
+- cooperative ownership;
+- public office;
+- founder equity;
+- debt organization;
+- salvaging an abandoned project;
+- political mandate;
+- rare expertise;
+- institutional alliance;
+- building a new settlement economy around the project.
+
+The game must not communicate that personal wealth is the only legitimate path to large-scale agency.
+
+# 5. Construction Difficulty
+
+A large carrier build requires:
+
+- design authority;
+- yard capacity;
+- energy;
+- materials;
+- component suppliers;
+- software and identity systems;
+- life-support ecology;
+- trained labor;
+- safety review;
+- financing;
+- political permission;
+- launch or orbital assembly logistics;
+- commissioning crew;
+- long-term maintenance plan.
+
+The player can influence all of these through different professions.
+
+Construction should be a chain of playable campaigns, not one progress bar.
+
+# 6. Control Surfaces
+
+## Direct embodied control
+
+Appropriate for:
+
+- piloting smaller craft;
+- local maneuvering;
+- emergency station work;
+- tools and repair;
+- EVA;
+- physical inspections.
+
+## Station control
+
+Appropriate for:
+
+- propulsion;
+- navigation;
+- thermal systems;
+- traffic;
+- medical coordination;
+- fabrication;
+- sensors;
+- defense.
+
+## Department coordination
+
+The player sets priorities and approves plans while qualified people execute details.
+
+## Vessel command
+
+The player or NPC command team selects mission intent, risk posture, route, emergency priorities and interdepartmental tradeoffs.
+
+## Governance
+
+Residents and institutions decide charter, budgets, rights, long-term purpose and leadership through the vessel's political structure.
+
+No single interface should flatten all five layers.
+
+# 7. Automation
+
+Automation should make large ships playable without implying that people are decorative.
+
+Automation can:
+
+- hold routine attitude and course;
+- balance utilities;
+- schedule maintenance;
+- detect faults;
+- run fabrication sequences;
+- coordinate traffic;
+- propose emergency responses.
+
+Automation cannot legitimately decide without bounded authority:
+
+- whose district loses atmosphere;
+- whether patients are abandoned;
+- whether residents are conscripted;
+- whether a sovereign craft is seized;
+- whether lethal force is used;
+- whether the ship changes its constitutional mission.
+
+Player settings may reduce mechanical workload, but not erase social consequence.
+
+# 8. Crew and Resident Agency
+
+Crew may:
+
+- disagree with commands;
+- report unsafe orders;
+- refuse unlawful work;
+- demand relief;
+- form unions;
+- leave when safely possible;
+- seek transfer;
+- vote or petition under the charter;
+- preserve professional ethics;
+- act independently during loss of command.
+
+Residents may not be treated as crew merely because they live aboard.
+
+Children, patients, guests and dependents retain distinct status and protection.
+
+# 9. Failure of Legitimacy
+
+A technically functional carrier may lose legitimate command through:
+
+- corruption;
+- repeated safety violations;
+- concealment of damage;
+- unlawful violence;
+- failure to honor rescue duties;
+- resident abuse;
+- command incapacity;
+- charter breach;
+- loss of confidence;
+- external court order;
+- succession dispute.
+
+Possible outcomes:
+
+- investigation;
+- recall;
+- mutiny with varied legitimacy;
+- command split;
+- departmental refusal;
+- external trusteeship;
+- secession of attached craft;
+- negotiated charter reform.
+
+# 10. Multiplayer Command
+
+A player group should be able to divide roles naturally.
+
+Possible arrangement:
+
+- one mission commander;
+- one navigation lead;
+- one engineering lead;
+- one harbor and deployment lead;
+- one care or habitation lead;
+- one governance delegate;
+- multiple independent carried-base captains.
+
+Absence and disconnection rules must:
+
+- delegate routine authority;
+- prevent one player from holding the carrier hostage;
+- protect owned property;
+- preserve character consent;
+- record emergency actions for later review;
+- allow command succession.
+
+# 11. Difficulty Presets Without Canon Breakage
+
+Groups may choose assistance levels:
+
+## Guided operations
+
+High automation, clear recommendations, forgiving schedules, broad delegation.
+
+## Professional operations
+
+More station work, deeper planning, realistic staffing and maintenance.
+
+## Command simulation
+
+Detailed authority, watch rotations, logistics, failures and political negotiation.
+
+## Continuity-hardcore
+
+Limited replacement, severe rescue delays, binding damage and demanding governance.
+
+All presets preserve the same world rules. They alter interface assistance, workload compression and failure tolerance—not whether physics or rights exist.
+
+# 12. Success Criteria
+
+The design succeeds when players can say:
+
+- “I mattered aboard the carrier before I owned anything.”
+- “Becoming qualified felt different from becoming powerful.”
+- “We built this with a society, not a crafting menu.”
+- “Command meant responsibility rather than omnipotence.”
+- “The ship kept living when one player logged out.”
+- “Owning part of the carrier created duties as well as privileges.”
+
+## Contract Maxim
+
+> **Make the carrier easy to dream about, early to enter, demanding to master, hard to own, monumental to build, and impossible to rule as though nobody else aboard were real.**
